          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 68-year-old postmenopausal woman fractures her hip after a simple fall from standing height. Her DEXA scan shows a T-score of −2.0. What is the correct diagnosis?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteopenia only, since her T-score is above −2.5</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The T-score of −2.0 falls in the osteopenia range by itself, but the fragility fracture overrides this. A fracture from a force that should not normally break a bone confirms established osteoporosis regardless of the T-score.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Established osteoporosis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A fragility fracture — a break from a force that normally would not fracture bone — confirms established osteoporosis, even when the T-score alone would classify as osteopenia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Normal age-related bone loss</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>A hip fracture from a simple fall from standing height is a fragility fracture, not a consequence of normal ageing. It indicates pathologically weakened bone requiring treatment.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteomalacia with pathological fracture</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Osteomalacia presents with abnormal biochemistry — low calcium, low phosphate, elevated ALP, and low vitamin D. Without such findings, a fragility fracture in a postmenopausal woman points to osteoporosis, not osteomalacia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The video explained two diagnostic pathways for osteoporosis: a T-score ≤ −2.5, or a fragility fracture (a break from minimal trauma such as a fall from standing height). A fragility fracture confirms established osteoporosis even if the T-score alone falls in the osteopenia range."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. Osteoporosis can be diagnosed by two routes: a T-score at or below −2.5, or a fragility fracture. When a fragility fracture occurs, the diagnosis is established osteoporosis regardless of the T-score."
                }
              ],
