          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>Which clinical sign is considered pathognomonic for rickets?</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>Which clinical sign is considered pathognomonic for rickets?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Frontal bossing of the skull</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Frontal bossing is a recognised feature of rickets but is not pathognomonic, as it can also occur in conditions such as hydrocephalus and achondroplasia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rachitic rosary at the costochondral junctions</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The rachitic rosary is characteristic of rickets but not pathognomonic. Similar costochondral beading can appear in other conditions such as scurvy.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Harrison's sulcus along the lower ribcage</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Harrison's sulcus results from diaphragmatic traction on softened ribs and is highly suggestive of rickets, but it is not designated as pathognomonic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Rachitic wrist with widened, flared metaphysis</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>The rachitic wrist is pathognomonic for rickets. The widening and flaring of the distal metaphysis directly reflects defective endochondral ossification at the growth plate, the hallmark of this disease.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The narration identified the rachitic wrist as the pathognomonic sign of rickets. While frontal bossing, rachitic rosary, and Harrison's sulcus are all important clinical features, none of them is exclusive to rickets."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. The rachitic wrist — widening and flaring of the distal metaphysis — is pathognomonic for rickets, directly reflecting disordered mineralisation at the growth plate."
                }
              ],
              "behaviour": {
