          "label": "<p>A patient presents with deep bone pain. Investigations show: serum calcium norma</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A patient presents with deep bone pain. Investigations show: serum calcium normal, serum phosphate normal, serum ALP markedly elevated (10× normal), PTH normal, vitamin D normal. Which condition best explains this biochemical profile?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteomalacia</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Osteomalacia typically shows low calcium, low phosphate, elevated ALP, elevated PTH, and low vitamin D. Normal calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D values exclude this diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Paget's disease of bone</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Paget's disease causes focal, intense osteoblastic activity that markedly elevates ALP, while calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D remain normal because the bone remodelling is localised rather than systemic.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Osteoporosis</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>In osteoporosis, all biochemical parameters including ALP remain within normal limits. The problem is bone density, not mineral regulation, so a markedly elevated ALP rules out this diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Nutritional rickets</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Nutritional rickets produces the same biochemical fingerprint as osteomalacia — low calcium, low phosphate, elevated ALP, elevated PTH, and low vitamin D — but even more exaggerated. Normal values for these parameters exclude rickets.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "Remember the pattern: Paget's disease shows dramatically elevated ALP with normal calcium, phosphate, PTH, and vitamin D. This isolated ALP elevation distinguishes it from osteomalacia (low Ca, low PO₄, low vitamin D) and osteoporosis (everything normal including ALP)."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Correct. An isolated, markedly elevated ALP with all other biochemical parameters normal is the hallmark of Paget's disease, reflecting intense but localised bone remodelling."
                }
              ],
